From: aiden build <build@aiden.local>
Subject: [PATCH] adb_wifi: auto-connect after pairing with retry (race-safe)

After pairing, the device switches to a connect port and advertises
_adb-tls-connect, but mDNS discovery must browse+resolve it before it lands
in sAdbSecureConnectServices. On a freshly-started server (the first
`adb pair` auto-forks the server) the registry is still empty when pairing
completes, so a single immediate connect races discovery and loses -- this
is why first-time pairing never auto-connected but a later pair (after the
discovery thread had populated the registry) did.

Retry the connect on a detached background thread with a 2s delay between
attempts (15 attempts, ~30s) until the service appears and connects. Each
attempt is deferred to the MAIN fdevent thread via fdevent_run_on_main_thread
(discovery owns sAdbSecureConnectServices there); a shared promise carries the
result back to the retry loop. The detached thread does not block the pairing
response returned to the user.

Aiden-local patch (post-pair auto-connect, retry against discovery race).
---
--- a/vendor/core/adb/client/adb_wifi.cpp
+++ b/vendor/core/adb/client/adb_wifi.cpp
@@ -16,7 +16,10 @@
 
 #include "adb_wifi.h"
 
+#include <chrono>
 #include <fstream>
+#include <future>
+#include <memory>
 #include <random>
 #include <thread>
 
@@ -31,6 +34,7 @@
 #include "adb_known_hosts.pb.h"
 #include "adb_utils.h"
 #include "client/adb_client.h"
+#include "fdevent/fdevent.h"
 #include "sysdeps.h"
 
 using adbwifi::pairing::PairingClient;
@@ -263,5 +267,40 @@
 
     // Write to adb_known_hosts
     write_known_host_to_file(device_guid);
-    // Try to auto-connect.
+
+    // Try to auto-connect after pairing. The device switches from the pairing
+    // port to a connect port and advertises _adb-tls-connect, but mDNS discovery
+    // must browse+resolve that service before it lands in sAdbSecureConnectServices.
+    // On a freshly-started server (the very first `adb pair` auto-forks the server)
+    // the registry is still empty here, so a single connect attempt races discovery
+    // and loses. Retry on a detached background thread with a delay between attempts
+    // until the service shows up and connects.
+    //
+    // adb_secure_connect_by_service_name() reads sAdbSecureConnectServices, which
+    // discovery mutates on the MAIN fdevent thread. We must NOT touch it from this
+    // pair service thread, so each attempt is deferred to the main thread via
+    // fdevent_run_on_main_thread; a promise carries the result back so we can decide
+    // whether to keep retrying.
+    std::thread([device_guid]() {
+        constexpr int kMaxAttempts = 15;                       // ~30s total
+        constexpr auto kRetryDelay = std::chrono::seconds(2);
+        constexpr auto kMainThreadWait = std::chrono::seconds(5);
+        for (int attempt = 0; attempt < kMaxAttempts; ++attempt) {
+            auto connected_promise = std::make_shared<std::promise<bool>>();
+            std::future<bool> connected = connected_promise->get_future();
+            fdevent_run_on_main_thread([device_guid, connected_promise]() {
+                connected_promise->set_value(
+                        adb_secure_connect_by_service_name(device_guid.c_str()));
+            });
+            if (connected.wait_for(kMainThreadWait) != std::future_status::ready) {
+                return;
+            }
+            if (connected.get()) {
+                return;  // Connected successfully
+            }
+            std::this_thread::sleep_for(kRetryDelay);
+        }
+        // Gave up after kMaxAttempts; discovery will auto-connect later if the
+        // service appears.
+    }).detach();
 }
